Non-saltatory conduction, on the other hand, occurs in non-meduated fibres, which do not have a mymiculum. In this type of conduction, the nerve impulse propagates continuously along the entire length of the axon. This process is slower and less efficient than saltatory conduction, as the ions must flow through the entire axon wall to propagate the nerve impulse.

In summary, saltatory conduction is characterized by the rapid, jerky propagation of the nerve impulse through the nodes of the meduated fibres, while non-saltatory conduction is characterized by the continuous, slower propagation of the nerve impulse along the entire length of the non-meduated fibres.

