What is the significance of axillary lymph node status in primary breast cancer?<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","The status of axillary lymph nodes is crucial in the diagnosis and treatment of early-stage breast cancer. Axillary lymph nodes are located in the upper thighs and help to filter fluids from the breast tissue. If breast cancer spreads to these lymph nodes, it is called axillary spread or ""tanodectomy."" Axillary lymph node status helps determine the stage of the cancer and influences the type of treatment needed.

Axillary lymph node status is assessed by evaluating the number of nodes that have cancer cells, which is called the number of positive nodes. The presence of positive nodes indicates that the cancer has spread beyond the breast tissue, which can affect the prognosis and treatment planning.

Positive axillary lymph nodes are associated with a higher risk of cancer recurrence and metastasis, which is the spread of cancer to other parts of the body. Therefore, the presence of positive nodes may lead to more advanced treatment options, such as more extensive surgery, radiation therapy, or hormonal therapy.

In some cases, the axillary lymph nodes may be tested for cancer cells before surgery through a procedure called sentinel lymph node biopsy. This involves injecting a radioactive tracer or a fluorescent dye into the breast tissue, which travels to the sentinel lymph nodes, which are the first lymph nodes that cancer cells would likely reach. The sentinel lymph nodes are then removed and tested for cancer cells. If the sentinel lymph nodes are negative, further lymph nodes may not need to be tested, reducing the risk of side effects and preserving breast tissue.

In conclusion, axillary lymph node status is an important prognostic factor in early-stage breast cancer, influencing the stage of the cancer and the subsequent treatment plan. Early detection and accurate staging of axillary lymph node involvement are crucial for optimal treatment outcomes and quality of life for patients with breast cancer.
