(b) **Forced expirpiratory volume in 1 second (FEV₁)**: Decreased due to the narrowing of the airways, making it harder for blood to flow out of the lungs.  
(c) **Plasma HCO₃⁻ concentration**: Increased due to respiratory alkalosis, which occurs when the lungs cannot excrete CO₂ efficiently.  
(d) **CO transfer factor (Tco₂)**: Normal because obstructive airways disease does not affect the diffusion of CO₂ across the alveolus-tissue interface.  

