The key difference between absolute asymmetric synthesis and other types of asymmetric synthesis lies in the level of enantiomeric excess and the mechanism of achieving it. In absolute asymmetric synthesis, the reaction is designed to produce only one enantiomer, while other types of asymmetric synthesis may rely on the use of chiral catalysts or auxiliaries to favor the formation of one enantiomer over the other. However, these methods often still produce a mixture of enantiomers, with the ee depending on the efficiency of the catalyst or auxiliary.

