","The molecular formula C4H10O can exhibit two types of isomerism: chain isomerism and functional group isomerism.

1. Chain isomerism: This type of isomerism occurs when the carbon atoms in the molecule are arranged in different orders. For example, in C4H10O, the molecule can have a straight chain (butane) or a branched chain (2-methylpropanol).

2. Functional group isomerism: This type of isomerism occurs when the molecule has the same molecular formula but different functional groups. In C4H10O, the functional group can be an alcohol (e.g., 1-butanol or 2-butanol) or an ether (e.g., diethyl ether).

Examples of isomers for C4H10O:

1. Butan-1-ol (straight chain alcohol)
2. Butan-2-ol (branched chain alcohol)
3. Diethyl ether (ether)

These isomers have the same molecular formula but different structures and properties due to their different arrangements of atoms and functional groups.
