","The terms ""isototopologues"" and ""isotopomers"" are used to describe variations in molecules based on isotopic substitution. Here’s the key difference:  
1. **Isototopologues**: These are molecules that differ only in the isotopic composition of their atoms. For example, \(^{12}\text{C}_2\text{H}_2\) and \(^{13}\text{C}_2\text{H}_2\) are isotopologues because they are both ethyne (acetylene) but differ in the carbon isotope (12C vs. 13C).  
2. **Isotopomers**: These are molecules that differ only in the isotopic composition of their atoms, but they also have the same molecular structure. For example, \(^{12}\text{C}_2\text{H}_2\) and \(^{13}\text{C}_2\text{H}_2\) are also isotopomers because they both have the same ethyne structure.  

Thus, **all isotopologues are isotopomers**, but not all isotopomers are isotopologues.  

\boxed{\text{Isotopoplogues} = \text{Isotopomers with same structure}}
