   - **1. \( C \) lies in one of the two half-spaces determined by \( H \)**:
     - Since \( y a_i \geq 0 \) for all \( i \), the hyperplane \( H \) is parallel to the cone \( C \).
     - The condition \( y b < 0 \) implies that \( b \) lies in the half-space where \( y \) is negative.
   - **2. \( b \notin H \)**:
     - The condition \( y b < 0 \) implies that \( b \) is not on the hyperplane \( H \).
   - **3. \( b \) lies in the other half-space determined by \( H \)**:
     - By the above, \( b \) lies in the half-space where \( y \) is negative, which is the other half-space determined by \( H \).

Thus, we have shown that for any point \( b \notin C \), there exists a hyperplane \( H \) passing through the origin and a nonzero linear function \( y \) satisfying the required conditions. 

\[
\boxed{\text{The separation of } C \text{ and } b \text{ is achieved by a hyperplane } H \text{ and a nonzero linear function } y \text{ satisfying the conditions above.}}
\]
