A polycrystalline aluminium alloy contains hard particles with a diameter of \(10^{-8}\) m and an average center-to-center spacing of \(6 \times 10^{-8}\) m in the slip planes. The shear modulus \(G\) of aluminium is 26 GN m\(^{-2}\), and the Burgers vector \(b\) is 0.286 nm. Estimate the contribution of these particles to the tensile yield strength \(\sigma_{yr}\) of the alloy.<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","The contribution to the tensile yield strength \(\sigma_{yr}\) of the alloy is calculated using the Hall-Oenswald equation:  
\[
\sigma_{yr} = \frac{G b}{d \left(1 - \frac{1}{2} \frac{d}{d_0}\right)}
\]  
where:  
- \(G = 26 \times 10^9\) N m\(^{-2}\) (shear modulus),  
- \(b = 0.286 \times 10^{-9}\) m (Burgers vector),  
- \(d = 6 \times 10^{-8}\) m (particle spacing),  
- \(d_0 = 10^{-8}\) m (particle diameter).  

Substituting the values:  
\[
\sigma_{yr} = \frac{26 \times 10^9 \times 0.286 \times 10^{-9}}{6 \times 10^{-8} \left(1 - \frac{1}{2} \frac{6 \times 10^{-8}}{10^{-8}}\right)}
\]  
\[
\sigma_{yr} = \frac{7.676 \times 10^3}{6 \times 10^{-8} \times 0.5} = \frac{7.676 \times 10^3}{3 \times 10^{-8}} = 2.5587 \times 10^{11} \text{ N m}^{-2}
\]  
Converting to MPa:  
\[
\sigma_{yr} = 2.5587 \times 10^{11} \text{ N m}^{-2} = 255.87 \text{ MPa}
\]  

Thus, the contribution of these particles to the tensile yield strength is \(\boxed{255.87 \text{ MPa}}\).
