","To calculate the recoil velocity of the Earth, we use the principle of momentum conservation. Momentum is a vector quantity, and the total momentum of a system remains constant unless an external force acts on the system.

1. Let the initial momentum of the system (cannonball + Earth) be \(P_{\text{initial}} = 0\), as both the cannonball and Earth are initially at rest.
2. Let the final momentum of the system be \(P_{\text{final}} = m_{\text{cannonball}} v_{\text{cannonball}} + m_{\text{Earth}} v_{\text{Earth}}\), where \(m_{\text{cannonball}} = 1 \text{ kg}\), \(v_{\text{cannonball}} = 90.0 \text{ m/s}\), \(m_{\text{Earth}} = 5.97 \times 10^{24} \text{ kg}\), and \(v_{\text{Earth}}\) is the recoil velocity of the Earth.

3. By momentum conservation, \(P_{\text{initial}} = P_{\text{final}}\), so:
   \[
   0 = m_{\text{cannonball}} v_{\text{cannonball}} + m_{\text{Earth}} v_{\text{Earth}}
   \]
   \[
   0 = (1 \text{ kg})(90.0 \text{ m/s}) + (5.97 \times 10^{24} \text{ kg}) v_{\text{Earth}}
   \]
   \[
