","The net chemical reaction during the discharging of a lead storage battery is:
\[ \text{Pb(s)} + \text{PbO}_2(\text{s}) + 2\text{H}^+(\text{aq}) + 2\text{SO}_4^{2-}(\text{aq}) \rightarrow 2\text{PbSO}_4(\text{s}) + 2\text{H}_2\text{O(l)} \]

**Explanation:**
1. **Anode (Reduction):** The lead electrode (Pb) oxidizes to form lead(II) ions (Pb²⁺), while the lead(II) sulfate (PbSO₄) is reduced to lead (Pb).
   \[ \text{Pb(s)} + \text{SO}_4^{2-}(\text{aq}) \rightarrow \text{PbSO}_4(\text{s}) + 2e^- \]

2. **Cathode (Oxidation):** The lead(II) oxide (PbO₂) electrode is reduced to lead(II) ions (Pb²⁺), while the sulfate ions (SO₄²⁻) are oxidized to sulfate (SO₄²⁻).
   \[ \text{PbO}_2(\text{s}) + 2\text{H}^+(\text{aq}) + 2\text{SO}_4^{2-}(\text{aq}) + 2e^- \rightarrow \text{PbSO}_4(\text{s}) + \text{H}_2\text{O(l)} \]

3. **Net Reaction:** Combining the two half-reactions and canceling out the electrons and Pb²⁺ ions, the net reaction is:
   \[ \text{Pb(s)} + \text{PbO}_2(\text{s}) + 2\text{H}^+(\text{aq}) + 2\text{SO}_4^{2-}(\text{aq}) \rightarrow 2\text{PbSO}_4(\text{s}) + 2\text{H}_2\text{O(l)} \]

Thus, during the discharging of a lead storage battery, lead and lead(II) oxide are converted into lead sulfate, and water is produced. The battery stores energy in the form of lead sulfate.
