A proton with mass \(1.67 \times 10^{-27} \, \text{kg}\) and charge \(1.60 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C}\) accelerates from rest in a uniform electric field of strength \(500 \, \text{N/C}\).  
(a) What is the magnitude of the acceleration of the proton?  
(b) How long does it take the proton to reach a speed of \(35,000 \, \text{m/s}\)?<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","(a) The proton is subject to a force \(F = qE\), where \(q = 1.60 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C}\) and \(E = 500 \, \text{N/C}\).  
\[
F = (1.60 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C})(500 \, \text{N/C}) = 8.00 \times 10^{-17} \, \text{N}
\]
Using Newton's second law, \(F = ma\), the acceleration is:
\[
a = \frac{F}{m} = \frac{8.00 \times 10^{-17} \, \text{N}}{1.67 \times 10^{-27} \, \text{kg}} = 4.76 \times 10^{10} \, \text{m/s}^2
\]
(b) The proton starts from rest (\(u = 0\)) and reaches a speed \(v = 35,000 \, \text{m/s}\).  Using the kinematic equation \(v^2 = u^2 + 2ad\), we solve for the distance \(d\):
\[
d = \frac{v^2 - u^2}{2a} = \frac{(35,000 \, \text{m/s})^2 - (0)^2}{2(4.76 \times 10^{10} \, \text{m/s}^2)} = 3.78 \times 10^{-3} \, \text{m}
\]
Now, using the kinematic equation \(v = u + at\), we solve for the time \(t\):
\[
t = \frac{v - u}{a} = \frac{35,000 \, \text{m/s} - 0}{4.76 \times 10^{10} \, \text{m/s}^2} = 6.35 \times 10^{-6} \, \text{s}
\]
Final answers:
(a) The magnitude of the acceleration is \(\boxed{4.76 \times 10^{10} \, \text{m/s}^2}\).  
(b) The time required is \(\boxed{6.35 \times 10^{-6} \, \text{s}}\).
